[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":109},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1794444101000,"id","ycpc4t6owv1zzkfv5pc9ajkli","title","AWS 推出更易用且默认更安全的新使用体验","originalTitle","Ease of use and secure defaults in the new AWS experience","summary","AWS 推出全新使用体验，以 AWS Builder ID、内置 MFA、AWS Organizations 管理项目及基础数据边界降低入门配置成本。账户默认使用单个 AWS Region，项目支出上限从每月 $20 起，达到上限后暂停服务以控制费用；用户可按需启用高级功能并直接接管组织，无需迁移或停机。","reason","source",{"_72":108},"links",{"_61":107},"publishedAt","2026-11-11T19:57:21.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-11T20:18:08.713Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[104,105,75,106],"score",43,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"x","readingMode","full","author","Keshav Narsipur","body",{"_100":-5,"_61":101,"_102":-5,"_103":36},"outline",[82,83,84,85,86],"relatedStories",[78],"topics",[68,69,70],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fW0AhlIFtD1Q","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_71":76,"_72":77},{"_71":74,"_72":75},{"_71":30,"_72":73},"slug","name","蓝队与运营","identity","身份与访问","cloud-native","云与原生",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"publicId","4eb9ba2a-b6cd-4258-a820-47924e38e125","AWS推出默认更安全的新使用体验",{"_11":98,"_88":99,"_89":93},{"_11":96,"_88":97,"_89":93},{"_11":94,"_88":95,"_89":93},{"_11":91,"_88":92,"_89":93},{"_11":87,"_88":45,"_89":90},"sec-5","text","level",3,"sec-4","Building for security and ease of use",2,"sec-3","Why the foundation matters","sec-2","The secure defaults behind the new experience","sec-1","What developers told us","zh","<p>Starting on <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/\">Amazon Web Services (AWS)</a></span> is now more straightforward and more secure by default than it’s ever been. Over the past several months, we rolled out a <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/aws-reimagines-the-getting-started-experience/\">new AWS experience</a></span> that changes how you start building. It brings together a set of sensible, secure defaults, and taken one at a time, it’s easy to miss the bigger picture of what we’re building and why.</p><p>When we first built how you get started on AWS, cloud was a new concept. It was a world where RSS and APIs were the exciting new thing. Twenty years later, we’ve come a long way and learned a lot about how people start, build, and grow on AWS.</p><p>So, before I get into the recent launches, let me state the vision plainly: <i>Make AWS straightforward to start on without giving up the depth, the pricing, or the security that make it the right place to grow.</i> You shouldn’t have to choose between getting started quickly and staying secure. The rest of this post builds out this idea and highlights the defaults that make it real.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\"><b>What developers told us</b></h2><p>For years, developers have told us a version of the same thing. They knew AWS was where they wanted to grow, but they wanted to start fast, not plan for scale on day one. The same depth that lets AWS grow with you also meant more to set up on day one. There’s a payment method to enter before you can start, a console built for experienced administrators to get oriented in, and permissions to configure before your app can run. That feedback is fair, and it’s a big part of why we keep building a better AWS. The new experience is our answer to that, and most of it comes down to sensible, secure defaults.</p><h2 id=\"sec-2\"><b>The </b>secure defaults behind the new experience</h2><p>The new AWS experience sets secure defaults for you, so your setup starts secure instead of something you harden later. The defaults are sensible ones, drawn from what we’ve learned about how people start on AWS. Here are a few and what they do:</p><p><b>Builder ID sign-in, with two-factor protection built in.</b> In the early days of AWS, you signed in as the account’s root user. You could set up separate identities for everyone, but that took real effort, so sharing one login was often the path of least resistance. We’ve learned a lot since then. <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/sign-in-builder-id.html\">AWS Builder ID</a></span> is a personal identity that represents you as an individual, and you use it to sign in with email and password or through a social login like Google or Apple. When you start working with others, each person signs in with their own Builder ID rather than sharing one set of credentials, so access is tied to a person, not passed around. It’s the straightforward path rather than the hard one. In addition to that, you can register multi-factor authentication (<span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/iam/features/mfa/\">MFA</a></span>, an authenticator app or security key) that applies to whichever sign-in method you choose. This used to require deliberate setup. Now it’s there from the start.</p><p><b>A managed organization with </b><span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/organizations/\">AWS Organizations</a></span><b> doing the heavy lifting behind the scenes</b>. The starting flow is straightforward: you sign up and land in a <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/create-project.html\">project</a></span> where you can start building right away. What I find interesting is what’s happening underneath. A project is more than an account. It pairs an account with its own spend limit and its own set of collaborators, so each project is a self-contained boundary with access scoped to just the people you invite. Rather than dropping you into a bare account, AWS creates and manages an organization for you, with your project as a member account inside it, and sensible guardrails already in place. As you grow, you add more projects—each its own member account—when you have work that needs its own budget or collaboration boundary, like a personal experiment kept separate from the beta you’re putting in front of your first users. This is the structure experienced teams land on eventually, usually after learning the hard way that putting resources with different budget or collaboration boundaries in the same account is tough to unwind later. The difference is we create the organization that enables this best practice for you from the start, so you’re set up for success well beyond day one without having to design or operate any of it yourself. When you’re ready for full control, you can <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/activate-advanced-features.html\">activate advanced features</a></span> in a single step and take over the organization directly, with no migration or downtime.</p><p><b>A data perimeter that grows with you</b>. This is another default that I really want people to notice. You start with a foundational <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_data-perimeters.html\">data perimeter</a></span> already in place, restricting access to your resources from principals outside your organization. It’s a secure starting point rather than the complete, multi-dimensional data perimeter a larger organization would eventually build out. But the direction is that your security boundary should grow with you as you succeed on AWS, and here a brand-new developer gets that baseline automatically, without knowing the term or writing a line of policy.</p><p><b>A single AWS Region by default.</b> Your resources run in one home Region to start, <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/project-regions.html\">assigned when you sign up</a></span> (aside from the handful of AWS services that are global by nature). That keeps your footprint simple and predictable: a smaller surface to secure and reason about, and a clear answer to where your data lives. When you need to operate more broadly, you can activate advanced features.</p><p><b>Spend limits that put you in control</b>. You can set a hard <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/create-spend-limit.html\">spend limit</a></span> on a project, starting at $20 a month, with notifications as you approach it. If a project reaches its limit, AWS pauses it rather than letting charges keep climbing, and you decide whether to raise the limit and resume. For an individual developer or a small team, that means no surprise bills: you set the ceiling, and no charges accrue past it without your say-so. You stay in control, and you can think big knowing the guardrails are already in place. A spend limit can also serve as an early signal. Unexpected spend is a classic indicator of inappropriate access or a runaway process, and because the project pauses at the ceiling you set, it also caps how far a cost surprise can go.</p><p><b>Roles set up for you, so IAM isn’t step one.</b> As a new developer, you want to spend your early days building and experimenting, not wiring up IAM permissions. <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-aws-iam-role-manager-rethinks-the-starting-point-for-iam-roles/\">IAM Role Manager</a></span> is on by default in the new AWS experience, so it creates and attaches the IAM roles your app needs as you build. Those roles start with the permissions an app typically needs to call other AWS services, enough to get you moving, not meant to last forever. As your app matures and tightening permissions matters, you take those roles over and right-size them, and by then you’ll know exactly what it needs. You focus on your app, AWS handles the plumbing.</p><h2 id=\"sec-3\"><b>Why the foundation matters </b></h2><p>Starting from a secure base changes things in two ways. For you, it means the environment you start in is the one you keep. When you’re ready to scale, you grow it rather than building a new one. For us, it means <i>secure by default</i> is something we can keep extending, because a structured, secure starting point gives us a place to add to. We’re able to turn on additional governance controls as a natural part of the experience. One example we’re already working on is setting account-level <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html\">Amazon S3 Block Public Access</a></span> protections for newly created environments. I’m not going to put a timeline here, but I want to be clear about the trajectory.</p><h2 id=\"sec-4\">Building for security and ease of use</h2><p>Ease of use and strong security are too often framed as opposites. Everything we’ve shipped here is an argument that they don’t have to be. You can give someone the fastest possible path to building something real and have that path be secure and cost-safe by default. The same experience makes the easy things easy, like connecting your coding agent in a single step or inviting a collaborator by email, without extra setup. Each of these is useful on its own, but they were all designed around the same idea, that the easy path and the secure path should be the same path.</p><p>If you’ve tried the new experience, I’d genuinely like to hear what resonated and what you’d want next, including how we can make activating advanced features feel more managed over time. If you haven’t yet, the fastest way to see these defaults in action is to <span><a href=\"https://signin.aws.amazon.com/signup?request_type=builderId\">create an account</a></span> and start your first project. We’re listening.</p><hr><p> \n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/89a5fb838bc6131a.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Keshav Narsipur\" width=\"120\" height=\"160\"> \n  </p><h3 id=\"sec-5\">Keshav Narsipur</h3><p>Keshav is Vice President of Identity, Cloud Governance, and Certificate Trust Services at Amazon Web Services. Keshav leads a global organization of engineers, product managers, and security professionals operating hyper-scale services that handle over 2 billion requests per second. His teams bridge the gap between developer velocity and enterprise security. Keshav’s organization is currently focused on pioneering new identity and governance capabilities to secure the generative AI era.</p>","zhKind","complete","加固","云原生","AWS","https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/ease-of-use-and-secure-defaults-in-the-new-aws-experience/","AWS Security Blog",{"_110":111,"_112":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
